@startuml main-workflow
' cumin works - main workflow (requirement level)
' Draft: the skeleton of the normal path. A note Qn is an open question, not a decision.
' Participants are only the actors of ../overview.md. No implementation details.
' The labels, the states of an issue, and what starts each action of cumin are in issue-states.md.
' Render: run scripts/render-diagrams.sh at the root of the repository.

autonumber

actor "Owner" as Owner
participant "cumin" as Cumin
participant Planner <<Agent>>
participant "Implementer" as Impl <<Agent>>
participant "Reviewer" as Rev <<Agent>>
database "Issue tracker\n(GitHub Issues)" as Tracker
database "Repository\n(GitHub)" as Repo

note over Cumin
  A resident program on the Host (Mac mini).
  launchd restarts it when it stops.
  Later: send a heartbeat, watched from another machine.
end note
/ note over Tracker
  May change to Jira or another tracker later.
  The source code stays on GitHub.
end note

== 1. Receive a requirement ==

Owner -> Tracker : write the requirement as an issue, add cumin/type/requirement
Owner -> Tracker : when done, add cumin/status/ready to the requirement issue
note right of Owner
  The only entry to cumin is an issue. No layout of the repository is assumed.
  Adding cumin/status/ready means "submitted" (a draft is not picked up).
  The Owner may keep requirement documents (for example requirements/) in the repository.
  Linked from the issue, they are context that the agents read.
end note

Cumin -> Tracker : poll for issues with cumin/type/requirement and cumin/status/ready
note right of Cumin
  cumin is resident and fetches work by itself.
  The Owner does not start it.
end note

== 2. Plan ==

Cumin -> Tracker : change the label of the requirement issue from cumin/status/ready to cumin/status/planning
Cumin -> Planner : request: split this requirement
Planner -> Repo : read the repository (with the documents that the requirement issue links)
Planner -> Tracker : create implementation issues as sub-issues (1 issue = 1 implementation task)
Planner -> Tracker : record the dependencies between the issues
Planner -> Tracker : add a provisional risk to each issue
note right of Planner
  Risk criteria:
  risk/low = a few lines whose effect is obvious.
  risk/high = a change that a revert cannot undo (database migration, deployment or CI setting,
  authentication or payment, side effect on an external service, public API contract, rules of cumin).
  risk/medium = everything else.
  Split by policies/issue-sizing.md
  (a risk/high change is isolated in its own smallest issue).
end note
Planner --> Cumin : plan done (the agent run ended)
Cumin -> Tracker : check that sub-issues exist and each has a risk

== 3. Check the plan ==

Cumin -> Tracker : change the label of the requirement issue to cumin/status/awaiting-owner-review
Cumin -> Owner : notify: the split needs a check
Owner -> Tracker : check the split and the risks (the Owner fixes them if needed)
Owner -> Tracker : if good, add cumin/status/ready to the implementation issues
note right of Owner
  cumin/status/ready means three things:
  the split is approved, the risk is final, and the implementation may start.
  The Owner always takes part in the risk (a start).
  It may go on only some sub-issues. When they are all closed and only
  sub-issues without the label remain, cumin asks for a check again.
end note

== 4. Implement and review ==

loop while an issue has cumin/status/ready, its dependencies are done, and AI resources are free
  note over Cumin
    AI resources are free = the usage of both quota windows of Claude Code is below its limit.
    Weekly window: the pace limit = target x min(1, (elapsed + lead) / 7 days), so the week is used evenly.
    5h window: the threshold of the current time band (higher when the Owner does not work).
    The target, the lead, the thresholds, and the time bands are in the settings file of cumin.
    cumin can read the usage (the result of a Claude Code run holds the usage and the reset time; measured).
  end note
  Cumin -> Tracker : choose the next issue that can start
  Cumin -> Tracker : change the label from cumin/status/ready to cumin/status/implementing
  note right of Cumin
    Changing the label is the claim. It comes before the request, so the same issue is never requested twice.
    The status label is on the implementation issue, always exactly one. cumin changes all of them (never an agent).
    An issue that already has a pull request continues from there (after the Owner sends it back or answers).
    The agent then gets a new session and reads the issue, the pull request, and the comments again.
  end note
  Cumin -> Impl : request: implement this issue
  Impl -> Repo : push a branch and open a pull request
  Impl --> Cumin : implementation done (the agent run ended)
  Cumin -> Repo : check that the pull request is open
  Cumin -> Tracker : change the label to cumin/status/awaiting-checks
  Cumin -> Repo : wait until every required check passes (on a failure, ask the Implementer for a fix; with no required check, it passes at once)
  note right of Cumin
    An open pull request alone does not mean the implementation is done.
    cumin decides from facts on GitHub, not from what an agent reports.
    While the checks run, no agent runs, so no quota is used.
  end note

  loop until no blocking comment is left (up to 3 rounds; the number will be tuned)
    Cumin -> Tracker : change the label to cumin/status/reviewing
    Cumin -> Rev : request: review this pull request
    Rev -> Repo : post the review (APPROVE when nothing blocks, REQUEST_CHANGES when a fix is needed)
    Rev --> Cumin : review done (the agent run ended)
    Cumin -> Repo : read the review of the current head commit
    opt a comment to fix
      Cumin -> Tracker : change the label to cumin/status/implementing
      Cumin -> Impl : request: fix the comments
      Impl -> Repo : push the fix
      Impl --> Cumin : fix done
      Cumin -> Tracker : change the label to cumin/status/awaiting-checks
      Cumin -> Repo : wait until every required check passes
    end
  end

  alt approved, risk/low
    Cumin -> Repo : merge (I6)
    Cumin -> Tracker : close the implementation issue once, when GitHub did not
  else approved, risk/medium or risk/high
    Cumin -> Tracker : change the label to cumin/status/awaiting-owner-review
    Cumin -> Owner : notify: the merge needs a decision
    Owner -> Repo : approve the head commit with a review
    Cumin -> Repo : merge at the next poll (I12)
    Cumin -> Tracker : close the implementation issue once, when GitHub did not
    note right of Owner
      To send it back, comment on the pull request and add cumin/status/ready to the implementation issue again.
      Whether risk/medium can move to an automatic merge is decided while running.
    end note
  else blocking comments left after 3 rounds
    Cumin -> Rev : request: sort out what is not decided
    Rev -> Repo : post a short report for the Owner
    Cumin -> Tracker : change the label to cumin/status/awaiting-owner-decision
    Cumin -> Owner : notify: a decision is needed
    Owner -> Repo : answer
    Owner -> Tracker : add cumin/status/ready to the implementation issue again
  end
  Cumin -> Tracker : after the merge, copy the remaining work (Follow-up and suggestions not taken) to the requirement issue as a follow-up note
  note over Owner, Cumin
    While waiting for the Owner, cumin goes on with other issues that do not depend on it.
    The Owner's "go ahead" for work is adding cumin/status/ready.
    The Owner's "merge it" is an approving review of the head commit (I12).
  end note
end

== 5. Acceptance ==

note over Cumin, Tracker
  Start: every sub-issue of the requirement issue is closed.
end note
Cumin -> Planner : request: check the acceptance
Planner -> Repo : check each item of Requirements of the requirement issue on the latest main
Planner -> Tracker : comment the result, the evidence, and the remaining work on the requirement issue
Planner --> Cumin : check done (the agent run ended)
Cumin -> Tracker : check that the comment exists, change the label of the requirement issue to cumin/status/awaiting-owner-review
Cumin -> Owner : notify: the requirement can be accepted
alt accept
  Owner -> Tracker : close the requirement issue (= accepted)
  note right of Owner
    Work from the follow-up notes that the Owner wants goes into a new requirement issue.
  end note
else send back
  Owner -> Tracker : add to the requirement issue, or add sub-issues
  Owner -> Tracker : add cumin/status/ready to the added sub-issues
  note right of Owner
    A send-back does not go through the Planner (a start).
    Back to "4. Implement and review"; when every sub-issue is closed,
    the acceptance notification comes again.
  end note
end

== Exception: an agent cannot go on ==

Cumin -> Tracker : change the label to cumin/status/awaiting-owner-decision
Cumin -> Owner : notify: blocked, a decision is needed
Owner -> Tracker : answer
Owner -> Tracker : add cumin/status/ready to the implementation issue again

== Exception: the quota reached its limit ==

Cumin -> Owner : notify: the quota reached its limit, so no new issue starts
opt the Owner allows using up the current 5h window
  Owner -> Cumin : allow: use up the current 5h window
  note right of Owner
    The allowance lasts until that 5h window resets. The 5h threshold becomes 100%.
    Nothing raises the pace limit of the weekly window.
    First, the Owner gives the allowance with a command on the Host (from outside through Tailscale and SSH).
    Later: a Discord bot, so that the allowance comes from Discord.
  end note
end
note over Cumin
  When the window resets, or when the limit rises above the usage, cumin starts issues again by itself.
end note

== Exception: no issue can go on ==

Cumin -> Owner : notify: waiting
note right of Owner
  First, cumin only notifies and does nothing else.
  Later: draft the next requirement issue from the requirement documents, and more.
end note

note over Owner, Cumin
  A notification only says "come and look". The conversation is in issues and pull requests.
  Notifications use a Discord webhook (replaceable later).
end note

note over Cumin, Rev
  The identities on GitHub are apart from the Owner, one GitHub App for each role (policy):
  cumin-core, cumin-planner, cumin-implementer, cumin-reviewer.
  The target repository (peppercheck) is public, so rulesets work on the Free plan.
  A ruleset limits updates of main to the Owner and cumin-core.
end note

@enduml
